# RPC services

Use an RPC service when another process needs typed request/reply operations. Define the wire types, declare a service, then expose async methods from a handler implementation.

## Define a service and one operation

<Example title="Add a typed RPC operation">

```rust
use serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};
use upwell::daemon::{Payload, handlers, rpc, service};

#[derive(Deserialize)]
struct AddRequest {
    left: i64,
    right: i64,
}

#[derive(Serialize)]
struct AddResponse {
    total: i64,
}
```

Declare the singleton that owns the operation.

```rust
#[service]
struct Calculator;
```

Add an inherent handler implementation. `Payload<T>` decodes the request body.

```rust
#[handlers]
impl Calculator {
    #[rpc]
    async fn add(&self, Payload(request): Payload<AddRequest>) -> AddResponse {
        AddResponse {
            total: request.left + request.right,
        }
    }
}
```

</Example>

Register or auto-discover the service in the daemon application configuration. The generated server descriptor collects all RPCs from its `#[handlers]` blocks; client support generates a typed client when enabled.

## Resolve a dependency per call

Add `Inject<T>` for a dependency that belongs to the request handling scope. Keep the payload as the one request body parameter.

<Example title="Create through an injected store">

```rust context
#[service]
struct Calculator;
```

```rust
use upwell::daemon::{Inject, Payload};
use upwell::Dep;

struct CreateCustomer {
    name: String,
}

struct Customer {
    name: String,
}

struct CreateCustomerError;

struct CustomerStore;

impl CustomerStore {
    async fn create(&self, request: CreateCustomer) -> Result<Customer, CreateCustomerError> {
        Ok(Customer { name: request.name })
    }
}
```

Accept the payload and the dependency in the same handler.

```rust
#[handlers]
impl Calculator {
    #[rpc]
    async fn create_customer(
        &self,
        Payload(request): Payload<CreateCustomer>,
        Inject(store): Inject<Dep<CustomerStore>>,
    ) -> Result<Customer, CreateCustomerError> {
        store.get().create(request).await
    }
}
```

</Example>

Use `Result<T, E>` when the caller needs a typed failure; return `T` directly for operations that cannot fail. RPC methods are async and may take no receiver or exactly `&self`; the shared service cannot take `self` or `&mut self`.

## Stream values only when the operation needs them

Use a request stream for client-supplied sequences and return a stream for subscriptions or long-lived results.

<Example title="Sum uploaded values and watch events">

```rust context
#[service]
struct Calculator;
```

```rust
use futures_core::Stream;
use futures_util::StreamExt;
use upwell::daemon::RequestStream;

struct Event;
```

Mark the operations in the existing service handler block.

```rust
#[handlers]
impl Calculator {
    #[rpc]
    async fn sum(&self, values: RequestStream<i64>) -> i64 {
        values.fold(0, |sum, value| async move { sum + value }).await
    }

    #[rpc]
    async fn watch(&self) -> impl Stream<Item = Event> {
        futures_util::stream::empty()
    }
}
```

</Example>

An RPC accepts at most one streaming input, and that stream is the complete request body, so do not combine it with `Payload<T>`. Add `#[rpc(stream)]` only when a concrete return type hides its streaming shape.

